1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,300
This is a look at what SEAVISTA measured,

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:07,033
a research project built
at FORTH-ICS and co-funded

3
00:00:07,033 --> 00:00:11,613
by Frontex under the Frontex
Research Grants Programme.

4
00:00:11,613 --> 00:00:16,118
Every result here was measured on one
workstation, with one graphics card.

5
00:00:16,118 --> 00:00:19,253
The traffic is two recorded
days of Danish waters.

6
00:00:19,253 --> 00:00:25,517
That is 6.37 million position
reports, from about 21,900 vessels.

7
00:00:25,517 --> 00:00:30,934
So every result that follows was
earned on real traffic, by one machine.

8
00:00:30,934 --> 00:00:34,342
SEAVISTA scores every vessel
with each report it sends.

9
00:00:34,342 --> 00:00:39,819
As first deployed, it carried about
1,440 vessel updates a second.

10
00:00:39,819 --> 00:00:44,972
Two fixes in how requests are batched
lifted that 4 times, to about 6,000.

11
00:00:44,972 --> 00:00:48,958
Grouped into batches, the same
machine carries 48 times that.

12
00:00:48,958 --> 00:00:55,262
At that rate the graphics card
draws 177 of its 575 watts.

13
00:00:55,262 --> 00:00:58,467
So the scoring keeps up with
busy traffic on one machine,

14
00:00:58,467 --> 00:01:01,595
with most of its power in reserve.

15
00:01:01,595 --> 00:01:06,398
When a score crosses its threshold,
an AI agent investigates the case.

16
00:01:06,398 --> 00:01:10,534
We sent 16 cases at once, on
parallel inference capacity.

17
00:01:10,534 --> 00:01:16,313
With one worker, the median case waited
455 seconds before it was opened.

18
00:01:16,313 --> 00:01:19,225
With 16 workers it waited under a second.

19
00:01:19,225 --> 00:01:21,867
Each investigation still
took about a minute,

20
00:01:21,867 --> 00:01:25,787
so more capacity is more
of the same workers.

21
00:01:25,787 --> 00:01:28,940
Every score is calibrated,
so it means what it says.

22
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:30,367
On held out traffic,

23
00:01:30,367 --> 00:01:33,867
the share of reports flagged
matches the share it is set to,

24
00:01:33,867 --> 00:01:36,371
within 1.3 percentage points.

25
00:01:36,371 --> 00:01:39,000
So the scoring layer
is set wide on purpose,

26
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,979
and how wide is a measured choice.

27
00:01:41,979 --> 00:01:45,231
The agent stands between
those flags and the officer.

28
00:01:45,231 --> 00:01:49,638
We ran it over two labelled sets of
ordinary traffic, three times each.

29
00:01:49,638 --> 00:01:54,327
Out of 72 and 69 cases, no
false alarm reached the officer.

30
00:01:54,327 --> 00:02:00,200
Across the three runs, 56 of the 58
cases got the same answer every time.

31
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:03,837
The two that changed are
cases no named reason covers.

32
00:02:03,837 --> 00:02:09,820
So the officer's queue holds real cases,
and the same case gets the same answer.

33
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:13,833
We timed each alert from the signal
arriving to an officer seeing it,

34
00:02:13,833 --> 00:02:15,569
with every queue counted in.

35
00:02:15,569 --> 00:02:18,853
Over 20 alerts, the
median was 84.3 seconds.

36
00:02:18,853 --> 00:02:21,900
The slowest took 156.3 seconds.

37
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:26,093
In that time the agent has gathered
the evidence and written the case up.

38
00:02:26,093 --> 00:02:31,357
So the officer starts each case already
written up, minutes after the signal.

39
00:02:31,357 --> 00:02:34,856
Every decision the agent makes
cites its reasons, by name.

40
00:02:34,856 --> 00:02:38,551
In every labelled case, each
reason that applied was cited.

41
00:02:38,551 --> 00:02:42,567
A person gives the final verdict on
every case in the officer's queue,

42
00:02:42,567 --> 00:02:44,436
and that was checked end to end.

43
00:02:44,436 --> 00:02:47,293
So any call can be traced
back to why it was made.

44
00:02:47,293 --> 00:02:50,567
The explanations were also
put to an open survey,

45
00:02:50,567 --> 00:02:53,070
with the same situation shown two ways.

46
00:02:53,070 --> 00:02:55,633
One version carried the full explanation,

47
00:02:55,633 --> 00:02:58,033
its reasons and what
would change the call.

48
00:02:58,033 --> 00:02:59,428
The other left it out.

49
00:02:59,428 --> 00:03:02,267
Which version each person saw was random,

50
00:03:02,267 --> 00:03:03,600
entry by entry,

51
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:06,980
over 23 entries and 319 ratings.

52
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:08,700
With the full explanation,

53
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:12,067
86.5 percent of ratings were confident,

54
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:14,556
clearly more than for
the same case without it.

55
00:03:14,556 --> 00:03:17,033
The survey also set two traps.

56
00:03:17,033 --> 00:03:22,030
One recommendation was deliberately wrong,
and one investigation account was made up.

57
00:03:22,030 --> 00:03:25,433
Both were rated as
confidently as the sound ones.

58
00:03:25,433 --> 00:03:29,855
An explanation raises confidence,
whether the call is right or wrong.

59
00:03:29,855 --> 00:03:33,167
The survey measured what each
feature does to confidence,

60
00:03:33,167 --> 00:03:35,916
and that lesson holds for any AI at sea.

61
00:03:35,916 --> 00:03:41,458
SEAVISTA is built as a tool in a person's
hands, with every reason in view.

62
00:03:41,458 --> 00:03:45,884
A single model was trained on all
four national AIS datasets together.

63
00:03:45,884 --> 00:03:49,567
On Danish water its error is 13.3 metres,

64
00:03:49,567 --> 00:03:53,792
against 38.8 for a model
trained on Danish water alone.

65
00:03:53,792 --> 00:03:56,033
The small datasets gain the most,

66
00:03:56,033 --> 00:03:59,068
because they borrow how vessels
move from the large ones.

67
00:03:59,068 --> 00:04:04,885
So a sea with little data of its own still
gets a model that knows how ships move.

68
00:04:04,885 --> 00:04:08,978
A new sea area is folded in with
one full refresh of the models.

69
00:04:08,978 --> 00:04:11,800
On one workstation that
takes under 4 hours,

70
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:14,704
and refitting one region
takes about 30 minutes.

71
00:04:14,704 --> 00:04:19,138
So adding a new sea is an
afternoon's work on one machine.

72
00:04:19,138 --> 00:04:23,004
Then the whole chain ran end to
end on the two recorded days.

73
00:04:23,004 --> 00:04:27,395
In the busiest window it ran
53 times faster than real time.

74
00:04:27,395 --> 00:04:30,900
That is at least 15 times
the rate the reports arrive,

75
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:33,120
with its resources capped on purpose.

76
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:37,878
So the whole chain runs far ahead
of the rate real traffic arrives.

77
00:04:37,878 --> 00:04:39,767
When a vessel goes quiet for a while,

78
00:04:39,767 --> 00:04:42,550
the tracker has to know
it when it reports again.

79
00:04:42,550 --> 00:04:46,831
It matches on motion alone, with
the vessel's own identity held back.

80
00:04:46,831 --> 00:04:53,724
Across 307,035 such gaps, it switched
identity 0.10 times per hour of track.

81
00:04:53,724 --> 00:04:56,767
With nineteen in every
twenty reports removed,

82
00:04:56,767 --> 00:04:59,807
it still holds a vessel
to within 3 to 5 metres.

83
00:04:59,807 --> 00:05:02,667
So a vessel that goes
quiet keeps its identity,

84
00:05:02,667 --> 00:05:05,833
and its path through
the gap is still known.

85
00:05:05,833 --> 00:05:08,800
SEAVISTA was built at FORTH-ICS,

86
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:14,333
in its Computational Vision
and Robotics Laboratory.
